2026-08-31T12:05Z claude note THE TWO LEVELS ARE RULED (spec/DECISIONS.md 2026-08-31) and this ticket's gates now declare one each. Fixture level runs wherever the gate runs, on the committable corpus; library level runs against the real library or a disclosed machine and cannot be committed. The relation is calibration, not coverage: the fixture level is where assertions run, the library level is what re-earns the fidelity of every fixture standing in for something real. R20's revalidate-against-the-real-dictionary clause stops being a special case and becomes the pattern that binds every surrogate here — the RSS surrogate, R32's reference machine, any scaled corpus. DESIGN 2.8 carries the rule at the head of the gate list; spec/README.md's goal tables carry the per-term reading in a decided-at column that bench/check_progress.py holds to a closed vocabulary
2026-08-31T12:24Z claude note THE GATES LOST THEIR REQUIREMENTS AND KEEP THEIR WORK (spec/DECISIONS.md 2026-08-31). R20, R21 and R26 were retired from the sheet on the criterion that what verifies a promise is not itself a promise, and R19 kept only its property clause. Nothing in this ticket's scope changes: the RSS gate now stands over constraint C3 rather than over R20, the golden gate decides R34 rather than restating R21, the fold gate protects R19's property, and the convergence harness is apparatus for R1 and R17 with no requirement of its own. What does change is the standing they can claim — a gate that does not run is no longer a half-kept PROMISE on spec/README.md, it is unbuilt work here, which is where it belonged. DESIGN 2.8's bullets are retitled accordingly
2026-08-31T13:38Z claude note R19's FOLD SWEEP INHERITS TWO SCRIPT CLASSES from R7's second tier, ruled 2026-08-31: Arabic and Hindi. The agreement clause is that every token the query normalizer produces is one the index normalizer can also produce, and both scripts break the assumptions a Latin-plus-Cyrillic sweep was built on — Arabic joins letters and drops short vowels, Devanagari combines marks and forms conjuncts, so normalization that is a no-op in Latin is not one there. Chinese lands differently: no word boundaries at all, and the platform ships a two-gram geometry the query side has to match. The codepoint sweep's coverage is a fixture-level question and belongs to this ticket; what it must cover is R7's, not this ticket's, so it is read from the sheet rather than restated
2026-08-31T14:05Z claude note R32's TWO BOUNDS ARE PINNED (spec/DESIGN.md 2.8, 2026-08-31), so the build-time gate has numbers to assert instead of a placeholder. Reference machine: laptop-class x86-64, four cores, no GPU, in the runtime the implementation ships — the class the feasibility run used, deliberately modest because a bound met only on the author's desktop promises nothing to anyone else. Records: SHOULD inside 30 minutes at the design point, MUST inside 1 hour. Body text: SHOULD inside 12 hours, MUST inside 24, which is what indexed today means once written down. The rule the section now states in general: a time bound with no machine attached is not a bound, so this gate reports the machine it ran on and a slower machine is outside the disclosure rather than a failure of the promise. The GPU host stands as a second configuration where the same bounds hold with room to spare, never as a substitute
--- body ---
## Context
Build the gate estate of DESIGN.md §2.8, which owns every threshold and
assertion (golden-set Jaccard, RSS budgets, soak constants, convergence
arithmetic) — this ticket carries the work and its ordering, not a second
copy of the numbers. Repo-side work in this repo's Makefile and bench/, not
upstream PRs.

The five gates sort into two piles:
- BUILDABLE NOW: the fold gate (fold_sweep.mjs today always exits 0, prints a
  WARNING, and crashes on any tree lacking normalizeForSearch — it needs the
  classification fallback, nonzero exit codes, and a writable built checkout,
  since none exists in-repo; stock >=v1.7.2 ships the fold, so against current
  upstream the gate runs green by right — the waiver keyed to PR #19's URL was
  deleted on its merge, 2026-08-27, ticket 0014); the golden gate once
  0029 delivers its fixture corpus; the RSS gate in its pre-machinery form —
  server-only against stock upstream, red-with-waiver (0011 measured
  2 084,9 MiB), the worker assertion joining once the scoped-issue machinery
  exists. Fixture informed by X3a (0025).
- THE ACCEPTANCE SPEC (served to the harness offer, 0032): the convergence
  harness and the soak assert counters, prefix arithmetic, drift,
  pipeline:idle, lease migration, and double-commits — observables of a
  ledger and conductor that exist only once the scoped issues' machinery is
  BUILT. They are written as executable specs now and run later; they also
  need a mock Zotero local API (everything in bench/ talks to live Zotero),
  which lands with them.

Flagged (Copilot rounds 2-3): the rss-gate deviates from R20's letter twice —
slow-suite placement vs "asserted on every check", and the synthetic
surrogate vs "against the 44.9 MB dictionary" (copyrighted library content a
public repo cannot carry; the real-document run is X3a on the author's
machine). Both readings are on DECISIONS.md's awaiting-ratification list —
build the gate either way, wire it per the ruling.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] fold gate in `make check` — green against stock >=v1.7.2, red-by-classification against pre-fold trees
- [ ] golden gate enforcing DESIGN §2.8's thresholds over 0029's pinned set
- [ ] rss gate asserting DESIGN §2.8's budgets against the synthetic monster (per the R20 ruling)
- [ ] convergence harness + soak written as the acceptance spec (0032); runnable once the machinery and the mock local API exist
- [ ] fold/golden/RSS(pre-machinery form) in the Makefile with honest red/waiver states
- [ ] the unit is the MUST clause, not the requirement: one assertion per clause,
      so `delivered` on spec/README.md becomes a count rather than a reader's
      judgement (absorbed from tracker 0400, dissolved 2026-08-31)
- [ ] R17's single MUST carries four assertions behind it — N of M, per stage, the
      most-recent-covered date, metadata-only counted with its reason — so the
      clause count is a floor and this one is written as four
